      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "It is becoming apparent that emphysema is partly driven by self-reactive T cells inducing inflammatory damage. Thus, T cells become targets for therapy similar to other autoimmune diseases. Costimulatory blockade therapy targets disease-specific T cells, rendering them ineffective by blocking a necessary costimulatory event on the T-cell surface. This therapy is tested here in mouse emphysema."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS & METHODS",
          "content": "Peptides representing contact domains of counter receptors LFA-1 and ICAM-1 were used as blockade therapy in elastase-induced emphysema."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "When administered during the first week after disease induction, blockade prevented lung destruction, reduced leukocyte infiltration and inhibited the decrease in T-cell CD4:CD8 ratio, also common in human emphysema."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Costimulatory blockade therapy can affect the progress of emphysema."
        }
      ]},
